%erg 0.1
Title: The promotion frequency gate counts path aliases as distinct projects
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---

2026-09-16T17:30Z claude created by the same run: 253 of 258 promotion candidates were one project counted under alias paths, so nothing was promoted
--- body ---
## Context

Found during the `/dream -home-haduong--claude` run of 2026-09-16.

`provenance.py candidates` returned **258** promotion candidates. Measured by
canonicalising the project slugs, **253 of them are a single project counted
twice or three times under different paths**:

| Same project, counted as several |
|---|
| `-home-haduong-Climate-finance` / `-home-haduong-CNRS-projets-actifs-climate-finance-het` / `-home-haduong-Climate-finance--superseded-20260913` |
| `-home-haduong-chemin-de-voix` / `-home-haduong-CNRS-projets-actifs-chemin-de-voix` |
| `-home-haduong-git-erg` / `-home-haduong-CNRS-code-git-erg` |
| `-home-haduong-CNRS-papiers-actif-livre-milliards-climat` / `-home-haduong-CNRS-projets-actifs-livre-milliards-climat` |
| `-home-haduong-Oeconomia-Climate-finance` / `-home-haduong-Oeconomia-Climate-finance--superseded-20260913` |

`memory/.project-aliases.json` holds exactly two mappings, neither of them one
of the families above. The skill documents the frequency gate as ">=2 distinct
**canonical** projects (path aliases collapsed)", so the gate is specified
correctly and implemented against an alias table that was never filled in.

Of the five candidates that do span two canonical projects, four belong to one
research programme (`chemin-de-voix` / `tracing-kieu`) and would fail the
context-independence gate; the fifth, `user_profile`, spans two **machines**
(`cadens`, `padme`), not two projects — a second alias class the table does not
model either.

So the true candidate count is between zero and four, against 258 reported.

## Why this is above the floor

The frequency gate is the only **mechanical** gate of the three; cost and
context-independence are agent judgement. A broken mechanical gate does not
merely add noise — it moves the whole decision onto judgement while presenting
itself as evidence, and the decision it gates *writes to the harness tier*,
which is resident in every session of every project. A project-local lesson
promoted there is paid for by every session forever.

Nothing was promoted on 2026-09-16: the run refused the pass rather than
exercise judgement 258 times on a gate it had just measured broken.

## Relevant files

- `memory/.project-aliases.json` — two entries, needs the families above
- `skills/dream/provenance.py` — `candidates`, and wherever canonicalisation is applied
- `skills/dream/SKILL.md` — steps 9 and 10 state the gate's contract
- `memory/.provenance.json` — carries the raw project lists

## Actions

1. Populate the alias table with the observed families. Prefer deriving them
   where the derivation is safe (a `--superseded-<date>` suffix is a rename of
   the same project) over hand-listing what will drift.
2. Decide and record how a **machine** slug is treated. `user_profile` seen on
   `cadens` and `padme` is one project on two machines, not cross-project
   evidence, and the current table has no concept for it.
3. Make the gate report its own health: print how many candidates survive
   canonicalisation versus how many were raw, so a future flood is visible in
   the run output rather than in a hand count.
4. Re-run `candidates` after the table is filled and record the real number in
   this ticket before any promotion is evaluated.

## Test

- A candidate whose project list is two aliases of one project does **not**
  appear in `candidates` output. Positive control: a candidate whose list is two
  genuinely distinct canonical projects still does.
- The alias table is exercised by test, not merely present — a fixture with a
  known alias pair and a known real pair, asserting one is collapsed and the
  other is not.
- `--superseded-<date>` collapses to its base, asserted directly.

## Verification

- [ ] `candidates` collapses every alias family observed on 2026-09-16
- [ ] A machine-scoped entry is classified deliberately, not by omission
- [ ] The run output states raw versus canonical candidate counts
- [ ] The real candidate count is recorded here before any promotion is applied

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The frequency gate counts projects, and a test proves it does not count paths
